Perfect 👍 — this is a very important EKS (AWS Kubernetes) concept for DevOps engineers.
Let’s go step-by-step in simple real-time explanation + commands so you’ll understand how to use IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA) in an EKS cluster.

🧠 1. What is IRSA (IAM Role for Service Account)?
Normally, EKS pods cannot directly access AWS services (like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, etc.) securely.

If you give AWS access using the node IAM role, all pods running on that node get permissions 😟 (that’s insecure).

So AWS created IRSA → it allows a specific Kubernetes service account to assume a specific IAM role, giving fine-grained permissions to only those pods.

👉 In short:

IRSA = Secure way to let specific pods access AWS services using IAM roles.

🏗️ 2. Real-time Example Scenario
Let’s say:

You have an EKS cluster named demo-eks.

You have a pod that uploads data to an S3 bucket.

You want only that pod (not others) to have permission to PutObject in S3.

⚙️ 3. Steps to Create and Use IRSA
Let’s go step by step.

🧩 Step 1: Enable OIDC provider for your EKS cluster
EKS uses OIDC (OpenID Connect) for IRSA authentication.

aws eks update-kubeconfig --name demo-eks --region ap-south-1
Then associate OIDC provider with the cluster:

eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider \
    --region ap-south-1 \
    --cluster demo-eks \
    --approve
✅ This creates an OIDC identity provider in IAM for your cluster.

🧾 Step 2: Create an IAM Policy (permissions for S3)
Let’s create a policy that allows only putting objects into a specific bucket.

Create a file named s3-policy.json:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::my-demo-bucket/*"]
    }
  ]
}
Now create the policy in IAM:

aws iam create-policy \
  --policy-name s3-putonly-policy \
  --policy-document file://s3-policy.json
🧙‍♂️ Step 3: Create an IAM Role for Service Account
Using eksctl (simpler way):

eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
  --name s3-writer-sa \
  --namespace default \
  --cluster demo-eks \
  --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::<your-account-id>:policy/s3-putonly-policy \
  --approve
✅ What happens here:

It creates a Kubernetes Service Account named s3-writer-sa in default namespace.

It creates an IAM role and links it to that service account using annotations.

🔍 Step 4: Verify the Service Account
Check the service account:

kubectl get sa s3-writer-sa -n default -o yaml
You’ll see something like this:

metadata:
  annotations:
    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::<account-id>:role/eksctl-demo-eks-addon-iamserviceaccount-default-s3-writer-sa-Role
✅ This means IRSA is active — pods using this service account will automatically assume that IAM role.

🐳 Step 5: Use this Service Account in a Pod
Create a pod YAML that uses this service account:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: s3-writer-pod
spec:
  serviceAccountName: s3-writer-sa
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: amazon/aws-cli
    command: ["sh", "-c", "aws s3 cp /etc/hostname s3://my-demo-bucket/"]
Apply it:

kubectl apply -f s3-writer-pod.yaml
🧾 Step 6: Verify Access
If you check your S3 bucket, you’ll find that the pod successfully uploaded its /etc/hostname file.
No access key/secret key used! — all permissions came from the IAM Role linked via IRSA. ✅

🧩 Summary Flow Diagram (conceptually)
Pod (uses serviceAccountName: s3-writer-sa)
     |
     ↓
Kubernetes Service Account (annotated with IAM Role ARN)
     |
     ↓
EKS OIDC Provider (trusted by IAM)
     |
     ↓
IAM Role (with attached S3 policy)
     |
     ↓
AWS S3 Bucket (PutObject allowed)
